On Earth, almost anywhere persistent liquid water exists, life—or evidence of it—tends to appear, even in deep rock, subglacial lakes, acidic pools, hypersaline brines, and hydrothermal systems.

But water alone isn’t sufficient. Life also needs:

* usable energy,
* chemical building blocks,
* enough stability and time,
* conditions that don’t destroy complex chemistry faster than it forms.

So water is less “the cause of life” than life’s operating medium: it dissolves materials, transports them, enables reactions, regulates temperature, and connects compartments.

The striking pattern is:

water + gradient + chemistry + containment + time → increasingly plausible life

And yes—that makes every persistent wet interface on Earth, and potentially elsewhere, worth investigating.